quickjs-ng ships five scripts as bytecode generated on a little-endian host:
its REPL and standalone loader (gen/repl.c, gen/standalone.c) and the
builtins Array.fromAsync, Iterator.zip, and Iterator.zipKeyed (builtin-*.h).
Its bytecode keeps operands and its checksum in host byte order, so on the
big-endian targets `qjs -i`, `qjs -c`, and any use or enumeration of those
builtins fail with "SyntaxError: checksum error". Regenerating the bytecode
would mean running a target qjsc during the build. Instead, embed all five
as source text (C23 #embed) and compile them at run time, on every target
alike; gen/ and builtin-*.h are no longer compiled. The three builtins are
therefore not source-stripped: their toString() shows their JavaScript.
A REPL that fails to start now exits 1, as upstream's loader did. No target
code runs at build time.

